buting to the rich literary and historical tradition of the Hellenistic period."}, {"sourceSlug": "agriopas", "sourceName": "Agriopas", "verb": "TRANSMITS", "targetSlug": "athletic-history", "targetName": "Athletic History", "context": "Agriopas's "Olympionicae" played a role in transmitting the detailed records of ancient Greek athletic history to subsequent generations of scholars."}], "places": [{"name": "Olympia, Greece", "role": "Subject of works"}, {"name": "Ancient Greece", "role": "Cultural context"}, {"name": "Roman Empire", "role": "Recipient of knowledge"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Agriopas", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "agriopas"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q64466", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agriopas"], "tags": ["Authors & Novelists", "textual-transmission", "cultural-diffusion", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 5, "significanceNarrative": "Agriopas holds notable significance as a recorder of ancient Olympic victors, a tradition crucial for establishing chronologies and celebrating cultural achievements in the classical world, influencing later encyclopedists like Pliny the Elder. His work, though lost, represents a vital, specialized contribution to ancient Greek historiography and sports history.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-09-25T07:04:42Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#36103918254", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Agriopas was a writer of ancient Greece mentioned by Pliny the Elder. He was the author of an account of the Olympic victors, called the Olympionicae. His exact date of birth is unknown.", "newValue": "Agriopas was an ancient Greek writer, likely active in the Hellenistic period, whose primary known work, the "Olympionicae," meticulously cataloged the victors of the ancient Olympic Games. Although his exact dates of birth and death remain unrecorded, his existence and authorship are attested by Pliny the Elder in his monumental "Natural History," positioning Agriopas as a significant, albeit obscure, contributor to classical historiography and athletic records. His work represents a crucial, if now lost, link in the chain of ancient scholarship dedicated to preserving the legacy of Greece's most celebrated Panhellenic festival.

The "Olympionicae" by Agriopas served as an invaluable chronicle, meticulously listing the names of champions across various disciplines and Olympiads. Such detailed records were not merely academic; they were essential for establishing chronologies, validating lineage claims, and celebrating civic pride in Greek city-states, where Olympic victories conferred immense prestige. While the original text of Agriopas's work has not survived, its citation by Pliny suggests it was a recognized and authoritative source for information regarding athletic history, likely drawing upon earlier sources like the lists compiled by Hippias of Elis or Aristotle's "Olympionikon Anagraphe."

The enduring legacy of Agriopas, despite the loss of his writings, lies in his contribution to the systematic preservation of historical data, particularly in the specialized field of athletic history. His work, alongside others like Phlegon of Tralles's "Olympiades," helped to codify and transmit the records of the Olympic Games, influencing later Roman encyclopedists and historians who sought to synthesize Greek knowledge. This tradition of detailed record-keeping laid foundational groundwork for modern historical methodology and the study of ancient sport."}, {"timestamp": "2026-09-25T07:04:42Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#36103918254", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "500 BCE"}, {"title": "Literary traditions and narrative storytelling", "type": "Idea", "year": "500 BCE"}], "newValue": ["The profound cultural significance of the Olympic Games in ancient Greece necessitated comprehensive record-keeping of its victors.", "The intellectual tradition of Hellenistic scholarship fostered the creation of specialized historical and encyclopedic works.", "The desire among Greek city-states to preserve and celebrate the achievements of their citizens spurred the documentation of athletic success."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-09-25T07:04:42Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#36103918254", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Created literary works of enduring influence", "type": "Idea", "year": ""}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks that shaped subsequent civili", "type": "Movement", "year": "Classical"}], "newValue": ["Agriopas's "Olympionicae" contributed to the systematic documentation of ancient Olympic history, influencing later classical scholars.", "His work served as a valuable, albeit now lost, source for Pliny the Elder's "Natural History,